WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.450
Welcome to Session eleven.

2
00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:05.275
Session ten ended on a problem.

3
00:00:05.275 --> 00:00:15.625
Our convolutional network found the hydrophobic core of a signal peptide on its own, which was a lovely result, and then global average pooling threw away where it found it.

4
00:00:15.625 --> 00:00:25.475
And that matters, because a hydrophobic stretch at the start of a protein is a signal peptide, while the same stretch in the middle is probably a transmembrane helix.

5
00:00:25.475 --> 00:00:28.100
The network knew the pattern occurred.

6
00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.150
It did not know where.

7
00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:36.475
Today we fix that, and in fixing it we arrive at the architecture that everything modern is built on.

8
00:00:36.475 --> 00:00:41.625
There are two questions in this session, and it is worth holding both from the start.

9
00:00:41.625 --> 00:00:46.325
The first is: how can a model use context from the rest of a sequence?

10
00:00:46.325 --> 00:00:48.625
We will see three answers.

11
00:00:48.625 --> 00:00:54.325
The convolutional one from Session ten, where context grows slowly as you stack layers.

12
00:00:54.325 --> 00:00:59.050
The recurrent one, where a memory is carried along the sequence step by step.

13
00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:05.000
And attention, where every position consults every other position directly, in one step.

14
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:13.300
The second question is different in kind: how do we learn useful representations when labels are scarce but sequences are abundant?

15
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:16.950
Because that is the actual situation in biology.

16
00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:24.750
We have hundreds of millions of protein sequences and we have, for most interesting properties, a few thousand labels at best.

17
00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:26.375
Three blocks.

18
00:01:26.375 --> 00:01:31.300
The recurrent network and the LSTM, applied to signal peptides.

19
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:35.150
Attention, Transformers and protein language models.

20
00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:39.650
And then what pretraining actually changed, measured on the same task.

21
00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:50.975
And signal peptides run through the entire session, as they have run through this course since Session four, because SignalP's thirty-year history happens to contain every architecture we have studied.

22
00:01:50.975 --> 00:01:57.150
Everything in this lecture is also written out in full on the Session eleven page of the course book.

23
00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:05.375
The page is the reference and this is the walk through it, so whenever a number or a name goes past too quickly, that is where to look it up.

24
00:02:05.375 --> 00:02:09.400
I will say which part of the page we are in as each block starts.

25
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:16.300
Here are the three answers side by side, because I want the shape of the session visible before we start.

26
00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:19.325
A convolutional network, Session ten.

27
00:02:19.325 --> 00:02:24.000
Each layer sees a small window, and stacking layers widens the view.

28
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:34.550
Context grows with depth, which means that to relate two positions a hundred residues apart you need a deep network, and the information passes through every layer in between.

29
00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:37.575
A recurrent network, which is Block one.

30
00:02:37.575 --> 00:02:46.200
The model reads the sequence one position at a time and carries a hidden state: a vector that summarises everything it has read so far.

31
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:49.325
Context arrives step by step, in order.

32
00:02:49.325 --> 00:02:51.850
Attention, which is Block two.

33
00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:58.600
Every position compares itself with every other position directly and decides which ones to listen to.

34
00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:03.450
Any distance, in one step, with no intermediate layers to pass through.

35
00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:13.050
Those three are the whole architectural story of modern sequence modelling, and each has a characteristic weakness, which I will ask you about at the end of the session.

36
00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:19.300
And then, written across the bottom of the slide, the second question: what do we do when labels are scarce?

37
00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:21.075
Hold that one.

38
00:03:21.075 --> 00:03:24.500
It turns out to have the same answer as the first.

39
00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:26.575
Block one.

40
00:03:26.575 --> 00:03:29.925
How can a model remember sequence context?

41
00:03:29.925 --> 00:03:35.250
On the page, this block is the section "How can a model remember sequence context?".

42
00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:49.225
We build a recurrent network, find out exactly how it fails, meet the LSTM that fixes it, and then apply the result to a real annotation problem: labelling which residues of a protein belong to a signal peptide.

43
00:03:49.225 --> 00:03:53.400
And the key result of this block is not about architecture at all.

44
00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.575
It is about direction.

45
00:03:56.175 --> 00:04:00.000
Here is the recurrent idea, and it is a simple one.

46
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.775
The model reads the sequence one residue at a time.

47
00:04:03.775 --> 00:04:13.500
At each step it has a hidden state, which is just a vector of numbers, and you should think of that vector as its memory: a summary of everything it has read so far.

48
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:16.300
At each position it does one thing.

49
00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:21.075
It takes the current residue and the old memory, and computes a new memory.

50
00:04:21.075 --> 00:04:25.925
New memory equals some function of this residue and the previous memory.

51
00:04:25.925 --> 00:04:27.825
Look at the picture.

52
00:04:27.825 --> 00:04:33.025
The blue arrows coming up from below are the residues entering, one per position.

53
00:04:33.025 --> 00:04:37.800
The orange arrows running left to right are the memory being carried along.

54
00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:46.525
And now the crucial detail, which is the same trick as Session ten's convolution in different clothing: it is the same weights at every position.

55
00:04:46.525 --> 00:04:51.500
One set of weights, applied again and again as the model walks along the sequence.

56
00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:54.450
That has a consequence worth stating.

57
00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:58.425
The number of weights does not depend on the length of the protein.

58
00:04:58.425 --> 00:05:07.425
One network handles a protein of fifty residues and a protein of five hundred, because it is the same step, repeated a different number of times.

59
00:05:08.025 --> 00:05:12.725
Let me put concrete sizes on that, because the shapes are what make it real.

60
00:05:12.725 --> 00:05:16.875
Each residue enters as an embedding of twenty-four numbers.

61
00:05:16.875 --> 00:05:23.150
An embedding is a lookup table: one row of trainable numbers for each of the twenty amino acids.

62
00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:32.150
Instead of representing leucine by a one-hot vector of nineteen zeros and a one, you give it twenty-four numbers that the network learns for itself.

63
00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:43.450
And as Session seven noted in passing, an embedding layer is nothing but a linear layer applied to a one-hot vector: multiplying a one-hot vector by a matrix picks out one row.

64
00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:46.975
The novelty is in the name, not the mathematics.

65
00:05:46.975 --> 00:05:50.475
The memory, the hidden state, has forty-eight numbers.

66
00:05:50.475 --> 00:05:53.125
So there are two weight matrices.

67
00:05:53.125 --> 00:06:00.500
W x h, which turns the incoming residue into something that can update the memory: forty-eight by twenty-four.

68
00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:05.900
And W h h, which carries the old memory forward: forty-eight by forty-eight.

69
00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:12.850
Those two matrices are the entire recurrent network, and their size does not change with the length of the protein.

70
00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:17.200
Forty-eight by twenty-four, and forty-eight by forty-eight, for anything.

71
00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:20.075
Now, how do you train it?

72
00:06:20.075 --> 00:06:25.225
You backpropagate, as in Session nine, but through every step the network took.

73
00:06:25.225 --> 00:06:32.575
If the protein has sixty residues, the gradient from the end has to travel back through sixty steps to reach the beginning.

74
00:06:32.575 --> 00:06:36.525
That procedure has a name: backpropagation through time.

75
00:06:36.525 --> 00:06:40.575
And if Session nine is fresh, you can already see the problem.

76
00:06:40.575 --> 00:06:49.225
At each step backwards, the gradient is multiplied by a factor involving the recurrent weight matrix and the derivative of the activation.

77
00:06:49.225 --> 00:06:53.500
Sixty steps means sixty such factors multiplied together.

78
00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:55.525
Put numbers on it.

79
00:06:55.525 --> 00:07:03.775
If the effective factor is nought point eight, then nought point eight to the power of sixty is one point five times ten to the minus six.

80
00:07:03.775 --> 00:07:08.825
The gradient reaching the first residue is a millionth of what left the last one.

81
00:07:08.825 --> 00:07:10.575
It vanishes.

82
00:07:10.575 --> 00:07:16.400
And if the factor is one point two, then one point two to the sixty is fifty-six thousand.

83
00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:20.950
The gradient explodes, and your training diverges on the first batch.

84
00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:25.200
This is exactly Session nine's vanishing gradient problem.

85
00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:28.600
The only difference is the direction it runs in.

86
00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:34.925
In Session nine the gradient had to travel back through layers; here it travels back along the sequence.

87
00:07:34.925 --> 00:07:38.125
Same multiplication, same catastrophe.

88
00:07:38.125 --> 00:07:42.825
So a simple recurrent network cannot learn long-range dependencies.

89
00:07:42.825 --> 00:07:49.625
In practice it remembers the last handful of positions and nothing more, which for a protein is not much use.

90
00:07:50.225 --> 00:07:57.125
The fix is the LSTM, the long short-term memory, and the central idea is one word: addition.

91
00:07:57.125 --> 00:08:06.750
In a simple recurrent network, the memory is recomputed from scratch at every step, which is why the gradient gets multiplied by something every time.

92
00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:18.025
In an LSTM there is a separate memory, called the cell state, which is carried forward and changed only by multiplying by a number between nought and one and then adding something.

93
00:08:18.025 --> 00:08:25.200
Because the path along the cell state is addition, the gradient flowing back along it is not being squashed at every step.

94
00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:33.475
The machinery for deciding what to add and what to keep is a set of gates, each a little network that outputs a number between nought and one.

95
00:08:33.475 --> 00:08:37.475
The forget gate decides how much of the old memory to keep.

96
00:08:37.475 --> 00:08:42.150
The input gate decides how much of the new candidate value to add.

97
00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:47.375
The output gate decides how much of the memory to expose as this step's output.

98
00:08:47.375 --> 00:08:49.600
Keep, add, expose.

99
00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:51.775
That is the whole thing.

100
00:08:51.775 --> 00:09:02.675
There is also the GRU, the gated recurrent unit, which does the same job with two gates and no separate cell state, and often performs about the same with fewer parameters.

101
00:09:02.675 --> 00:09:09.175
One honest qualification, which I will repeat on the next slide: this mitigates vanishing gradients.

102
00:09:09.175 --> 00:09:11.425
It does not abolish them.

103
00:09:11.425 --> 00:09:20.825
The additive path works as long as the forget gates stay close to one, and how long a dependency an LSTM can actually learn depends on the task.

104
00:09:21.425 --> 00:09:27.950
This slide is the inside of the cell, and I want to walk the one line that matters rather than every arrow.

105
00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:34.575
In the diagram, blue is a sigmoid gate, which outputs between nought and one and therefore acts as a valve.

106
00:09:34.575 --> 00:09:40.000
Green is a new candidate value, computed from the input and the previous output.

107
00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:43.625
And the orange line running across is the cell state.

108
00:09:43.625 --> 00:09:46.675
Follow the orange line from left to right.

109
00:09:46.675 --> 00:09:49.700
It is multiplied by the forget gate.

110
00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:52.275
Then something is added to it.

111
00:09:52.275 --> 00:09:54.400
And then it continues.

112
00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:56.900
That is all that happens to it.

113
00:09:56.900 --> 00:10:00.125
No squashing function sits on that path.

114
00:10:00.125 --> 00:10:02.300
Nothing compresses it.

115
00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:15.600
Which is exactly why the gradient can flow back along it over many steps: going backwards along an addition does not shrink anything, and going backwards along a multiplication by a forget gate near one does not shrink much either.

116
00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:23.825
Compare that with the simple recurrent network, where the memory went through an activation function at every step and was shrunk every time.

117
00:10:23.825 --> 00:10:27.525
The GRU, on the right, has no separate cell state.

118
00:10:27.525 --> 00:10:37.650
Its update gate z mixes the old state and the new candidate directly, and its reset gate r decides how much of the old state the candidate is even allowed to see.

119
00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:47.550
And a reassurance about this kind of diagram: in the notebook the gate equations are written out by hand and checked against PyTorch's nn dot LSTMCell.

120
00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:50.900
The maximum difference is zero point zero.

121
00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:55.650
There is no hidden magic; it really is these few lines of arithmetic.

122
00:10:56.250 --> 00:11:02.025
One more architectural idea, and this is the one that matters most for today's biology.

123
00:11:02.025 --> 00:11:08.225
A forward LSTM, reading left to right, knows at each position only what came before.

124
00:11:08.225 --> 00:11:12.150
At residue three it has seen residues one, two and three.

125
00:11:12.150 --> 00:11:13.850
That is all.

126
00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:20.625
A bidirectional LSTM runs two separate LSTMs: one left to right, one right to left.

127
00:11:20.625 --> 00:11:23.950
At every position their outputs are combined.

128
00:11:23.950 --> 00:11:28.800
So every position sees both what came before it and what comes after it.

129
00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:33.475
There is a cost, and it is a real constraint rather than a technicality.

130
00:11:33.475 --> 00:11:37.225
You can only do this if you have the whole sequence already.

131
00:11:37.225 --> 00:11:43.075
For annotation, which is our task today, that is fine: the protein is sitting there in full.

132
00:11:43.075 --> 00:11:45.875
For generation it is impossible.

133
00:11:45.875 --> 00:11:52.925
If you are producing a sequence one residue at a time, there is no future to read, because you have not written it yet.

134
00:11:52.925 --> 00:12:01.150
That constraint comes back in Block two as the difference between GPT and BERT, and the two cases are exactly analogous.

135
00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:03.425
Hold on to that trade.

136
00:12:03.425 --> 00:12:06.700
It is the same trade twice in one session.

137
00:12:07.300 --> 00:12:11.300
Now the biology, which we have been circling since Session four.

138
00:12:11.300 --> 00:12:17.750
A signal peptide is an address at the beginning of a protein that sends it into the secretory pathway.

139
00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:24.225
In a eukaryotic cell, into the endoplasmic reticulum; in bacteria, across the inner membrane.

140
00:12:24.225 --> 00:12:31.350
Günter Blobel received the Nobel Prize in nineteen ninety-nine for working out that proteins carry such addresses.

141
00:12:31.350 --> 00:12:34.575
Structurally it has three regions, in order.

142
00:12:34.575 --> 00:12:39.375
An n-region at the very start, which is short and positively charged.

143
00:12:39.375 --> 00:12:45.050
Then an h-region, the hydrophobic core, which is the part Session ten's filter found.

144
00:12:45.050 --> 00:12:52.025
Then a c-region, which is more polar, and which ends at the cleavage site where an enzyme cuts the signal peptide off.

145
00:12:52.025 --> 00:13:02.050
And today's task is per-residue annotation: for each of the first seventy residues of a protein, say whether that residue is part of a signal peptide or not.

146
00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:05.450
Notice this is not the task Session ten did.

147
00:13:05.450 --> 00:13:11.075
Session ten asked one question per protein; here we want a label per position.

148
00:13:11.075 --> 00:13:20.025
Which means, in Session ten's vocabulary, that we want equivariance rather than invariance: shift the sequence and the labels should shift with it.

149
00:13:20.025 --> 00:13:24.650
That is why a model that reads the sequence in order is the right tool.

150
00:13:25.250 --> 00:13:31.175
Before modelling, look at the data, which is a good habit and in this case genuinely beautiful.

151
00:13:31.175 --> 00:13:39.125
These are sequence logos of experimentally supported signal peptides, aligned on the cleavage site, which is the dashed line.

152
00:13:39.125 --> 00:13:46.750
The height of a stack is how much information that position carries, and the height of a letter is how often that residue occurs there.

153
00:13:46.750 --> 00:13:48.800
Read it from the right.

154
00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:54.725
Just before the cleavage site you see small residues at positions minus three and minus one.

155
00:13:54.725 --> 00:14:04.750
That is a real, named rule, the minus-three minus-one rule: the enzyme that cuts needs small residues at those two positions, so alanine and glycine dominate there.

156
00:14:04.750 --> 00:14:10.175
Move left and you see leucines filling the h-region, which is the hydrophobic core.

157
00:14:10.175 --> 00:14:17.600
And in bacteria the n-region, at the far left, is rich in lysine and arginine, the positively charged residues.

158
00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:20.800
One honest observation about the figure.

159
00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:37.225
The left-hand columns look blurred, and there is a reason: signal peptides differ in length, and the logo is aligned on the cleavage site, so a column some distance to the left mixes residues from the h-region of a short signal peptide with residues from the n-region of a long one.

160
00:14:37.225 --> 00:14:40.575
The n-region is therefore smeared, not weak.

161
00:14:40.575 --> 00:14:47.625
Note also that the two logos have different vertical scales, so do not compare their heights directly across panels.

162
00:14:48.225 --> 00:14:55.200
The dataset, and two decisions in it that I would like you to notice, because both are Session eight's lessons applied.

163
00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:58.675
Four thousand nine hundred and ten proteins.

164
00:14:58.675 --> 00:15:01.550
The first seventy residues of each.

165
00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:11.350
First decision: only signal peptides with experimental evidence are used, those with the evidence code ECO zero zero zero zero two six nine.

166
00:15:11.350 --> 00:15:13.450
Why does that matter?

167
00:15:13.450 --> 00:15:19.725
Because a great many signal peptide annotations in UniProt were themselves produced by SignalP.

168
00:15:19.725 --> 00:15:30.475
If we trained on those, we would be teaching our model to imitate SignalP's predictions, including its mistakes, and then congratulating ourselves when it agreed with SignalP.

169
00:15:30.475 --> 00:15:35.400
That is circular, and it is a mistake that is easy to make without noticing.

170
00:15:35.400 --> 00:15:40.350
Second decision: transmembrane proteins are included as negatives.

171
00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:46.875
And they are deliberately hard negatives, because a transmembrane helix is also a hydrophobic stretch.

172
00:15:46.875 --> 00:15:56.950
If your negatives are all soluble cytoplasmic proteins, your model only has to detect hydrophobicity, which is too easy and will fail in the real proteome.

173
00:15:56.950 --> 00:16:11.200
And the split is by MMseqs2 clusters, Session eight's homology partitioning, with the signal-peptide fraction kept at about nought point three nine, nought point three nine and nought point four zero across training, validation and test.

174
00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:15.075
Now the result, and it is unusually clean.

175
00:16:15.075 --> 00:16:20.175
Four models, same data, measured with the Matthews correlation coefficient.

176
00:16:20.175 --> 00:16:22.700
A plain window-based model.

177
00:16:22.700 --> 00:16:28.575
Then a simple recurrent network: MCC nought point six three one when forward only.

178
00:16:28.575 --> 00:16:30.875
Then an LSTM.

179
00:16:30.875 --> 00:16:35.550
And then a bidirectional LSTM: nought point eight two zero.

180
00:16:35.550 --> 00:16:49.625
And in terms of area under the precision-recall curve: window nought point six eight one, RNN nought point seven seven zero, LSTM nought point seven six seven, bidirectional LSTM nought point nine two zero.

181
00:16:49.625 --> 00:16:54.900
Look carefully at those four numbers, because the interesting thing is where the jump is not.

182
00:16:54.900 --> 00:17:02.775
Going from the simple recurrent network to the LSTM: nought point seven seven zero to nought point seven six seven.

183
00:17:02.775 --> 00:17:04.725
Essentially nothing.

184
00:17:04.725 --> 00:17:10.325
All that gate machinery, which is the clever part of the architecture, bought us nothing here.

185
00:17:10.325 --> 00:17:16.475
Going from forward to bidirectional: nought point seven six seven to nought point nine two zero.

186
00:17:16.475 --> 00:17:17.875
Enormous.

187
00:17:17.875 --> 00:17:23.600
So on this task, the architecture that matters is not the one with the sophisticated memory.

188
00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:27.475
It is the one that reads the sequence in both directions.

189
00:17:27.475 --> 00:17:29.375
Why would that be?

190
00:17:29.375 --> 00:17:32.100
Think about it before the next slide.

191
00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:37.525
What does a model at residue three actually need to know, and when does it become available?

192
00:17:38.125 --> 00:17:44.900
Here is the answer, and it is one of those results that is obvious once stated and would never have occurred to you.

193
00:17:44.900 --> 00:17:48.025
Consider residue three of a protein.

194
00:17:48.025 --> 00:17:52.575
A forward model, at that point, has seen residues one, two and three.

195
00:17:52.575 --> 00:17:55.450
Typically methionine and two others.

196
00:17:55.450 --> 00:17:58.875
That is the entire evidence available to it.

197
00:17:58.875 --> 00:18:02.050
But what distinguishes a signal peptide?

198
00:18:02.050 --> 00:18:04.625
The hydrophobic h-region.

199
00:18:04.625 --> 00:18:07.650
And the h-region has not happened yet.

200
00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:11.150
It is at positions seven to fifteen or so.

201
00:18:11.150 --> 00:18:16.850
The evidence that this residue belongs to a signal peptide arrives after the residue.

202
00:18:16.850 --> 00:18:21.850
A forward model is being asked to make a decision before the information exists.

203
00:18:21.850 --> 00:18:24.650
And you can measure exactly that.

204
00:18:24.650 --> 00:18:32.000
Of the first five residues of real signal peptides, the forward LSTM mislabels seventy-six per cent.

205
00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:35.700
The bidirectional one mislabels twelve per cent.

206
00:18:35.700 --> 00:18:46.750
Now look at the middle of the signal peptide, residues sixteen to twenty-five, where the h-region has already been seen by both models: nought point two zero against nought point two three.

207
00:18:46.750 --> 00:18:47.825
A tie.

208
00:18:47.825 --> 00:18:52.875
The forward model is not worse there, because by then it has the evidence too.

209
00:18:52.875 --> 00:18:57.175
And there is a mirror-image error, which is just as diagnostic.

210
00:18:57.175 --> 00:19:10.750
On proteins that have no signal peptide at all, the forward model wrongly calls the N-terminus a signal peptide: eleven point eight per cent of residues one to ten, against five point three per cent for the bidirectional model.

211
00:19:10.750 --> 00:19:12.725
Of course it does.

212
00:19:12.725 --> 00:19:22.300
The first few residues of many proteins look plausible, and only the absence of an h-region afterwards rules it out, which the forward model cannot yet see.

213
00:19:22.300 --> 00:19:31.350
So the lesson generalises well beyond this task: before choosing an architecture, ask where in the sequence the evidence for your label actually lies.

214
00:19:31.950 --> 00:19:36.450
Which brings us to the real tool, and one component we have not met.

215
00:19:36.450 --> 00:19:45.650
SignalP version five uses a bidirectional LSTM, which is what we just built, and then puts a conditional random field on top of it.

216
00:19:45.650 --> 00:19:49.700
A conditional random field is a structured output layer.

217
00:19:49.700 --> 00:20:07.125
The difference from what we have done is this: our model labels each residue independently, so nothing stops it producing a label sequence that is biologically impossible, for instance signal peptide for residues one to five, then not, then signal peptide again for residues eight to twelve.

218
00:20:07.125 --> 00:20:14.850
A conditional random field scores the whole label sequence at once, and it learns which transitions between labels are plausible.

219
00:20:14.850 --> 00:20:22.625
So it strongly prefers a single contiguous signal peptide starting at residue one, because that is what signal peptides are.

220
00:20:22.625 --> 00:20:33.550
It is the same idea as PHD's second level from Session seven, which took the per-residue predictions and cleaned up the implausible segments, done properly and trained jointly.

221
00:20:33.550 --> 00:20:41.850
A good general principle hides in here: if your labels have structure, say that structure in the model rather than hoping the model infers it.

